finding: 1. src/cli/publish-lock.ts:32–43; src/cli/publish.ts:540–548 — The lock fixes simultaneous publishes in one physical checkout but not the one-app provisioning race. Copy the same pending project to two directories (or hosts); both locks succeed, both target the same Fly app, and both can enter pinned flyctl first-deploy provisioning with empty volume inventories. Nothing locks the shared Fly app during that phase. src/cli/publish-flow.node.ts:413–436 uses one fixture path for both callers and cannot catch this. 2. src/cli/publish-lock.ts:35,64–70; src/cli/publish-fly.ts:96–101,273–285 — The stale-owner check tracks only the parent CLI PID, not its flyctl subprocess. On Unix, killing just the Shallot process while synchronous flyctl is still running does not kill the child. A retry sees ESRCH, removes the lock and can enter provisioning while the original flyctl still runs, recreating the race even from one checkout. src/cli/publish-flow.node.ts:441–453 simulates an exited PID that never launched a provider child; it does not test this crash case.
